# foreach-binding-vs-assignment — does FOREACH bind its variable, or assign to it?

STATUS: OPEN — needs a VENDOR PROBE. Raised by pjb (2026-08-30) on
reading slice 20: "foreach — are we sure that's how foreach behaves in
bricscad and autolisp? (did we run probes? or is it clearly specified
so?)".

Answer to the question as asked: NO on both counts. We did not probe it,
and the specification says the OPPOSITE of what the engine does.

## What clautolisp does

    (if (find-dynamic-binding name (current dynamic frame))
        (set-variable name element context)      ; ASSIGN
        (bind-dynamic-variable name element context))  ; BIND

i.e. it ASSIGNS when the name already has a dynamic binding ANYWHERE in
the chain, and only binds afresh otherwise. Measured, in this engine:

  1. loop variable is a /-local of the same function   => 3      (clobbered)
  2. loop variable is a /-local of the CALLER          => 3      (clobbered)
  3. loop variable is a global                         => OUTER  (preserved)
  4. name bound nowhere before                         => nil    (scoped)

Case 2 is the sharp one: a FOREACH inside a callee overwrites a LOCAL OF
ITS CALLER, reachable only because AutoLISP is dynamically scoped.

Case 3 disagrees with cases 1 and 2 for no reason a user could predict: a
global survives only because it lives in the NAMESPACE rather than in a
dynamic frame, so FIND-DYNAMIC-BINDING misses it. That asymmetry is an
artefact of the implementation, not a rule anyone chose.

## What the specification says

autolisp-spec, Special Form Entry: FOREACH —

  Evaluation Rules: "for each element of the resulting list, BIND it to
  name"
  Side Effects: "REBINDING name for each iteration"

and chapter 7 on documentation scoping is explicit that a "foreach
iteration variable that rebinds the name carries its own documentation
slot THAT IS RESTORED WHEN THE REBINDING FRAME IS POPPED".

Under those words, case 1 must yield BEFORE, not 3.

## What is verified

Nothing. The spec entry's own Availability section says: "AutoCAD 2026:
documented (per Autodesk reference page). BricsCAD V26: presumed
compatible (no contradicting page found). Status: AutoCAD documented;
BricsCAD presumed-both pending Phase 4 verification." And the Autodesk
page it cites documents FOREACH's purpose, not its scoping.

So the engine's behaviour rests on neither a probe nor a normative
sentence.

## Not caused by the compiler work

Slice 20 (2.0.21) open-coded FOREACH and preserved this exactly --
deliberately, by calling the same BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE the interpreter
calls, which is the rule that keeps compiled and interpreted evaluation
in step. Compiling it did not change it and could not have revealed it;
pjb asking did.

## Next step

probes/sources/probe-foreach-scope.lsp is written and registered in the
manifest. It reports the four cases above plus an empty list, an empty
body and the return value, and is phrased so the ANSWER says which
semantics the engine has without the reader needing to know the engine.
It needs a machine with AutoCAD and BricsCAD -- `make probe`.

## What to do with the answer

  - If the vendors BIND: clautolisp is wrong in cases 1 and 2, and the
    fix is one line in BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE (always bind in the frame
    FOREACH pushes). The compiler needs no change at all, because it
    calls that function. The equivalence corpus already contains both
    cases, so they will simply start reporting the other answer.
  - If the vendors ASSIGN: the spec's wording is wrong and should be
    corrected, and case 3 is still an inconsistency to settle -- a global
    ought to behave like any other prior binding.
  - If they DISAGREE with each other: it is a dialect divergence, which
    is what the dialect machinery exists for.

The one outcome that should not stand is the present one: an
implementation nobody checked, contradicting a specification nobody
checked it against.

## ANSWER FROM AUTOCAD (2026-08-30) — clautolisp is wrong

probe:suite:autocad:windows, AutoCAD on the Windows runner:

    case                                  AutoCAD    clautolisp
    /-local of the same function          BEFORE     3
    /-local of the CALLER                 BEFORE     3
    global                                OUTER      OUTER
    bound nowhere before                  nil        nil
    empty list                            BEFORE     BEFORE
    return value                          (30 nil nil) (30 nil nil)

AUTOCAD BINDS. The prior value is restored when the loop ends, in both
the same-function and the caller's-local cases -- exactly as the
specification says ("bind it to name", "rebinding name for each
iteration", "restored when the rebinding frame is popped").

clautolisp ASSIGNS, and so clobbers a local of the function AND of its
caller. It matches neither AutoCAD nor our own spec. The spec was right
and the implementation was wrong.

The two engines agree on everything else: the empty list, the unbound
name, and the return value in all three shapes.

BricsCAD has not answered yet (see
issues/open/probes-suite-crashes-bricscad.issue) but its answer cannot
make clautolisp's current behaviour correct for --strict or --autocad,
which are the dialects AutoCAD defines.

## The fix, when taken

One line in BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE: always bind in the frame FOREACH
pushed, instead of assigning to whatever the chain already has.

    (defun bind-foreach-variable (name element context)
      (bind-dynamic-variable name element context))

THE COMPILER NEEDS NO CHANGE. Slice 20 open-coded FOREACH by CALLING
this function rather than restating the rule, so compiled and interpreted
loops move together by construction. The equivalence corpus already
contains both affected cases and asserts that the two agree, not what
they agree ON, so it keeps passing and starts describing the new
behaviour.

What does need changing is any test that PINS the old values, and the
chapter-7 documentation-scoping text should be re-read to confirm it now
matches the code rather than contradicting it.

If BricsCAD turns out to ASSIGN, this becomes a dialect divergence rather
than a plain fix, which is what the dialect machinery is for.

## FIXED in 2.0.22, on AutoCAD's evidence (pjb: "You may take already the
## one-line foreach fix on AutoCAD's evidence")

BIND-FOREACH-VARIABLE now binds, unconditionally, in the frame FOREACH
pushed. clautolisp matches AutoCAD on every probed case:

    case                            AutoCAD   clautolisp now   was
    /-local of the same function    BEFORE    BEFORE           3
    /-local of the CALLER           BEFORE    BEFORE           3
    global                          OUTER     OUTER            OUTER
    bound nowhere before            nil       nil              nil

THE COMPILER NEEDED NO CHANGE, as predicted: slice 20 open-coded FOREACH
by CALLING this function rather than restating the rule, so the compiled
fork moved the moment the interpreter did.

WHAT THIS EXPOSED. All 31 suites passed BOTH BEFORE AND AFTER the change.
The equivalence corpus asserts that compiled and interpreted evaluation
AGREE -- true either way -- and nothing asserted WHAT they agreed on. So
nothing would have noticed a silent revert, and nothing had noticed the
original defect. Four tests now pin the values against AutoCAD's measured
answers, including the global case, which used to pass FOR THE WRONG
REASON: the old assignment path missed a global only because
FIND-DYNAMIC-BINDING does not look in the namespace, an accident rather
than a rule.

BRICSCAD IS STILL UNMEASURED (probes-suite-crashes-bricscad.issue). If it
turns out to ASSIGN, that is a dialect divergence to encode rather than a
reason to revert: AutoCAD defines --strict and --autocad, and the
specification says bind.


CONFIRMED AGAINST BRICSCAD TOO -- 2026-08-31, both platforms
------------------------------------------------------------

This was closed on AUTOCAD's evidence alone; BricsCAD had never
answered, because the probe suite would not LOAD there (see
probes-suite-crashes-bricscad.issue, and the two wrong guesses it
took). With the offending syntax moved into strings read at run time,
BricsCAD ran the whole suite on macOS and on Windows, and agrees with
AutoCAD on every scope case:

  case                                AutoCAD  BricsCAD  clautolisp
  loop variable is a /-local          BEFORE   BEFORE    BEFORE
  loop variable is the CALLER's       BEFORE   BEFORE    BEFORE
  loop variable is a global           OUTER    OUTER     OUTER
  bound nowhere before the loop       nil      nil       nil
  empty list -- no iteration          BEFORE   BEFORE    BEFORE

BOTH VENDORS BIND. The 2.0.22 fix was right, and is now right against
two engines rather than one. The behaviour clautolisp had before it --
ASSIGN when the name was already bound anywhere in the chain, which
wrote a caller's local and left the last element in it -- matched
neither vendor and neither reading of the specification.

Raw evidence in the repository:
  probe-results/autocad/ms-windows/20260831T130043Z/
  probe-results/bricscad/ms-windows/20260831T130116Z/
  probe-results/bricscad/macos/20260831T140019Z/

ONE DIVERGENCE DID TURN UP, and it is not about binding: a FOREACH with
NO BODY returns THE LIST in BricsCAD and NIL in AutoCAD. New ticket:
issues/open/foreach-empty-body-return-diverges.issue.
